  "passage": "…t is underestimating the probability of success of the company's trial of its immune deficiency drug. \"We believe Street's POS [probability of success] is too low for mavorixafor's Ph3 in chronic neutropenia (CN) creating a favorable skew ahead of the 2H27 readout.\" Wells Fargo upgrades Seagate to overweight from equal weight Wells says investors should buy the dip. \"With the recent market pullback and increasing confidence in what we view as a path to +$50 EPS + significant cap return capacity ahead, we upgrade STX to OW w/ a $1,100 PT (~20x CY28 P/E; was $900).\" Morgan Stanley reiterates SpaceX as overweight Morgan Stanley says the release of Grok 4.5 is an important catalyst for SpaceX. \"Key question remains whether SpaceXAI will be able to combine Grok + Cursor to create compelling, differentiated enterprise products that aren't easily replicated by competing labs.\" Morgan Stanley reiterates Nvidia as overweight Morgan Stanley says the stock remains a top idea following a meeting with management. \"This week, we hosted a number of investor meetings in California as part of an NDR with Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and head of IR Toshiya Hari. The meetings were upbeat, as expected, with the company describing \"accelerating growth rates\" even as revenues approach the $100 bn per quarter mark.\" Raymond James initiates BrightSpring as outperform Raymond James says the healthcare company has a differentiated offering. \"We are initiating coverage of BrightSpring Health Services (BTSG) with an Out…",
  "statement": "Key question remains whether SpaceXAI will be able to combine Grok + Cursor to create compelling, differentiated enterprise products"
 },
 {
  "id": "G214",
  "article_id": "gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "For a long time, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) was priced above $200 per share. Now, it has fallen below that after a few days of heavy selling pressure. The stock is down around 16% from its highs and is up a mere 6% for the year. That's a pretty disappointing result for most investors since Nvidia has been such a strong stock pick over the past few years.\nHowever, I still think the stock still has a bright future. In fact, I think it could easily rise over 50% to reach $300. But when might that occur",
  "passage": "…k associated with the industry is struggling.\nNvidia is not isolated from that, which is why it has sold off so much. However, time and time again, the hyperscalers have told investors that the risk of underspending far outweighs the risk of overspending. The market may be growing frustrated by lofty capital expenditures on data centers, but the hyperscalers aren't planning on slowing down anytime soon.\nDuring its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\nThat sounds like further revenue growth ahead, and that's exactly what Wall Street analysts are projecting as well. For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%. Those are strong growth rates and will likely lead to major profits as well.\nFor fiscal 2028, ending January 2028, the average analyst projects $12.76 in earnings per share. I think that's a conservative estimate, because the analyst community has consistently underprojected Nvidia's growth.\nIf we use that figure and value the stock at a reasonable earnings multiple of 25, the projected share price at the end of fiscal 2028 is $319. As a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 per share sometime in late 2027.\nThat's 50% upside in about a year and a half, which is a great return in a short time frame . Furthermore, most of this growth is already known because management likely has …",
  "statement": "For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%."
 },
 {
  "id": "G012",
  "article_id": "gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a",
  "title": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reach",
  "passage": "…t in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 w…",
  "statement": "the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1"
 },
 {
  "id": "G475",
  "article_id": "gnews:621e1b678a037049c86a70af",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Micron: Which Is the Better AI Semiconductor Stock to Own for the Next 5 Years?",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-04",
  "article_opening": "Artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure stocks are still one of the biggest driving forces in the market. While Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) helped lead the early charge, other stocks, like Micron ( MU +4.20% ) , have greatly outperformed the AI leader over the past year.\nLet's dive into both AI stocks to see which one looks set to outperform over the next five years.\nNvidia was the first big AI winner, and the advantages the company established remain in place today. Meanwhile, despite its massiv",
  "passage": "…in expanded to 84.6% from 37.7%. That led its adjusted earnings per share (EPS) to skyrocket from just $1.91 a year earlier to $25.11.\nThe revenue growth and margin expansion are coming from a huge supply-demand imbalance within the memory market. The company is one of the big three DRAM makers -- along with Korean companies SK Hynix and Samsung -- and derives around three-quarters of its revenue from DRAM and a quarter from NAND (flash). AI is driving memory demand, as GPUs are packaged with high bandwidth memory (HBM), a special form of DRAM that helps reduce latency and optimize performance, and AI training data is being stored in enormous solid state drives (SSDs) that use flash memory.\nRival SK Hynix has predicted that the DRAM market will remain supply-constrained through at least 2030, while Micron and its rivals have begun to lock in long-term contracts for up to five years for the first time ever. This should help reduce some of the extreme cyclicality the industry has seen in the past.\nOn a forward P/E basis, Micron looks very cheap, trading at only 6 times analyst estimates for fiscal 2027 (ending August 2027). The question is how long the memory cycle will last and whether there has been enough of a structural shift in the market to keep prices high.\nI think the easy answer here is that Nvidia will outperform Micron over the next five years. Micron is still a cyclical stock until proven otherwise, and five years is a long time for a cycle to last. Meanwhile, Nvidia …",
  "statement": "Rival SK Hynix has predicted that the DRAM market will remain supply-constrained through at least 2030"
 },
 {
  "id": "G149",
  "article_id": "gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4",
  "title": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of th",
  "passage": "…m the supported capacity. This approach offers AI cloud operators a viable means to finance large installations, which have traditionally been challenging to fund, especially for emerging companies lacking the balance sheet strength typically required for capital-intensive projects.\nThe initiative reflects a significant shift in the AI market, where demand is increasingly focused not only on training large models but also on running them in production for enterprises, software providers, and AI-native businesses that require constant access to computing resources for inference, fine-tuning, and related workloads.\nTwo companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs. These projects exemplify the scale of infrastructure Nvidia aims to facilitate through this financing model, which is linked to future usage rather than relying solely on conventional long-term commitments.\nNvidia describes these facilities as DSX AI factories tailored to customer demand in various regions. For users of AI infrastructure, this model promises faster access to computing capacity, eliminating delays associated with site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware installation typical of traditional buildout…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G488",
  "article_id": "gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc",
  "title": "Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-25",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models requi",
  "passage": "…or, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.",
  "statement": "management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales"
 },
 {
  "id": "G236",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "… years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\nBeyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032. Firmus will still need to identify a datacenter provider or build their own datacenters before they can deploy GPUs.\nWe have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.\nNvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\nThere are many more deals in the works and we believe that many of the clusters announced to be built in partnership with Nvidia will later be confirmed to be built atop an Nvidia backstop. In the next section, we will explore how this revenue backstop will affect Nvidia’s balance sheet and income statement.",
  "statement": "AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity"
 },
 {
  "id": "G390",
  "article_id": "gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a",
  "title": "DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above ",
  "passage": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]\nU.S. export controls bar Chinese designers from accessing the most advanced overseas foundries and high-bandwidth memory, posing a major manufacturing hurdle [2]\nDeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.\nThe project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2] . The …",
  "statement": "DeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]"
 },
 {
  "id": "G113",
  "article_id": "gnews:bdcb0ed0921c2e9ae241083c",
  "title": "Palantir’s NVIDIA AI Collab Could Be a Game-Changer That Marks a Turn in the Stock",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Palantir shares surged 18% in a week as investors rotate from semis into AI software.\nA new deal using Nvidia's Nemotron to build government AI models squeezes short sellers and may justify Palantir's 145x trailing P/E multiple.\nDespite Palantir's 85% revenue growth quarter, Nvidia may offer better value.\nShares of Palantir ( NASDAQ:PLTR ) have been bouncing back, now up close to 18% in a week, and it might be a good idea to step out of the way if you're short or are hanging onto bearish put opt",
  "passage": "Palantir shares surged 18% in a week as investors rotate from semis into AI software.\nA new deal using Nvidia's Nemotron to build government AI models squeezes short sellers and may justify Palantir's 145x trailing P/E multiple.\nDespite Palantir's 85% revenue growth quarter, Nvidia may offer better value.\nShares of Palantir ( NASDAQ:PLTR ) have been bouncing back, now up close to 18% in a week, and it might be a good idea to step out of the way if you're short or are hanging onto bearish put options. Dr. Michael Burry recently announced that he'd covered half of his short position around a week ago, a move that's proved incredibly well-timed. As investors pile back into the AI software plays and away from the semiconductors, I do think that Palantir stands out as a name that…",
  "statement": "Palantir shares surged 18% in a week as investors rotate from semis into AI software."
 },
 {
  "id": "G347",
  "article_id": "nvblog:hugging-face-lerobot-models-frameworks-open-robotics",
  "title": "NVIDIA and Hugging Face Bring New Models and Frameworks to LeRobot for the Open Robotics Community | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "New LeRobot integrations give developers open access to NVIDIA Isaac GR00T 1.7, Isaac Teleop, datasets and robotics workflows, with NVIDIA Cosmos 3 integration planned to bring frontier world models to open robotics development.\nOpen source AI has shown how quickly developers can innovate when models, data and tools are shared. Robotics has the same opportunity, but advancements in physical AI development can still be gated by costly and fragmented resources, from large datasets and robot founda",
  "passage": "…cross the open robotics community.\n“Open source is how a field turns advanced research into something people can study, adapt and build on,” said Thomas Wolf, cofounder and chief science officer at Hugging Face. “With NVIDIA Isaac GR00T 1.7 and Isaac TeleOp in LeRobot today, robotics developers can use shared models, data and workflows to train and evaluate robots in the open. And with NVIDIA Cosmos 3 planned next, the community will have a path to bring frontier world models into that same collaborative loop.”\nAn Open Pipeline for Robot Foundation Models\nHugging Face LeRobot is an open source robotics library for training, running and sharing robot datasets, models, policies and workflows. NVIDIA’s continued partnership with Hugging Face connects NVIDIA’s 3 million robotics developers with Hugging Face’s 16 million AI builders, expanding access to frontier physical AI tools through open workflows.\nBringing NVIDIA physical AI capabilities into LeRobot gives developers a common way to collect and standardize data, train and fine-tune robot foundation models, evaluate performance and deploy models through open workflows. The integrations include:\nNVIDIA Isaac Teleop , an open source framework for robot data collection, helps developers capture high-quality human demonstrations from external devices using standardized, interoperable formats, then expand and share datasets with the community, all directly in LeRobot.\nNVIDIA Isaac GR00T 1.7 , the first open and commercially viable robot foundation model, makes it easie…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA’s continued partnership with Hugging Face connects NVIDIA’s 3 million robotics developers with Hugging Face’s 16 million AI builders"
 },
 {
  "id": "G119",
  "article_id": "gnews:1740f3733eedc34d8bac9b65",
  "title": "MicroLED manufacturer nabs Nvidia director to drive AI data center push",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "By Ben Wodecki\nAMS-Osram, an Austrian semiconductor manufacturer of light and sensor solutions, poached a networking director from Nvidia.\nAshkan Seyedi had served as Nvidia’s lead for technical product marketing on its co-packaged and pluggable interconnect solutions since 2021. In a LinkedIn post , Sayedi revealed he’s taking on the role of VP and GM of AMS-Osram’s optical interconnect business line.\nThe firm, whose headquarters is located on the grounds of an old Austrian castle in Premstätte",
  "passage": "…illion .\nMicroLEDs have become increasingly seen as an attractive alternative technology to power data center interconnects. Traditional copper links have a limited reach of around two meters, while optical links such as fiber optics can reach longer distances, but at the expense of high power consumption and lower reliability.\nMicrosoft is among those exploring potential microLED links, with its Mosaic prototype interconnect system capable of maintaining high network speeds with further reach while using far less power.\nThose efforts were initially detailed in a paper published last September, but have since matured via a collaboration with Taiwanese fabless semiconductor company MediaTek. The pair plans to launch microLED-based data center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027 .\nAMS-Osram’s efforts, meanwhile, will see work toward developing microLED optical interconnects for inside the rack as well as chip-to-chip communication between graphic processing units (GPUs) and high-bandwidth memory, CEO Aldo Kamper told investors during its Q1 2026 earnings call back in May.\n“I’m looking forward to meeting everyone and contributing to the exciting journey ahead,” Seyedi said. “Together we'll define and create the next front of optical interconnect solutions. More to come.”\nPrior to joining Nvidia, Sayedi was a senior research scientist at Hewlett Packard Enterprise (HPE), where he worked on a commercial process design kit (PDK) for silicon photonics.",
  "statement": "The pair plans to launch microLED-based data center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027"
 },
 {
  "id": "G177",
  "article_id": "gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587",
  "title": "Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks rais",
  "passage": "….\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\" SemiAnalysis said.\nThat leaves Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\nThe delays \"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\"\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of t…",
  "statement": "That leaves Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google"
 },
 {
  "id": "G120",
  "article_id": "gnews:aad624be9de524b95e766c96",
  "title": "Forget Nvidia: This Infrastructure Upstart Is The Real Backdoor AI Winner",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) , the world's largest producer of data center GPUs, is the linchpin of the booming AI market. Most of the world's top AI companies use its GPUs to train their large language models (LLMs), and Nvidia locks in those clients with its proprietary software.\nNvidia still has a bright future, but it's already the world's largest company with a market cap of $4.7 trillion. So if you're looking for bigger gains, it might be smarter to seek out smaller, faster-growing AI companies ",
  "passage": "…e why this Australian AI infrastructure stock might outperform Nvidia and the other AI market leaders over the next 12 months.\nWhat does IREN do?\nIREN was originally a Bitcoin ( BTC -0.57% ) miner called Iris Energy, which only used renewable energy in its mining operations. But in 2024, it rebranded itself as IREN and expanded into the AI infrastructure market by buying Nvidia's H100 and H200 GPUs.\nIn 2025, IREN allocated all of its capital, logistics, and power infrastructure toward expanding its AI and high-performance computing (HPC) data centers rather than its Bitcoin mining operations. Last November, it secured a 5-year, $9.7 billion AI cloud contract with Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) . This May, it signed a $3.4 billion contract for AI cloud services with Nvidia.\nThose two mega-deals turned IREN into one of the market's hottest \" neocloud \" stocks, attracting massive interest from retail and institutional investors alike, including Leopold Aschenbrenner's AI-oriented Situational Awareness hedge fund. IREN continues to mine Bitcoin, but most of its growth now comes from its booming AI business.\nWhat's next for IREN?\nFrom fiscal 2025 (which ended last June) to fiscal 2028, analysts expect IREN's massive deals with Microsoft and Nvidia to boost its revenue from $510 million to $6.44 billion. They also expect its adjusted earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization (EBITDA) to soar from $270 million in 2025 to $4.89 billion in 2028,…",
  "statement": "This May, it signed a $3.4 billion contract for AI cloud services with Nvidia"
 },
 {
  "id": "G284",
  "article_id": "gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512",
  "title": "CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek, is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nBig Tech leadership is shifting. The Magnificent 7 and Broadcom weakened in June, while the broader market held up better than expected and Micron surged.\nInvestors should respect both the opportunity and the risk. Micron’s growth, margins, and role in AI memory are impressive, but its ra",
  "passage": "… a number that it would signal a market top. Apple hit $1 trillion in 2018; now there are 17 companies in the trillion-dollar club.\nIs Micron the new Nvidia?\nTwo years ago, investors kept asking if it was too late to buy Nvidia . I wrote about this on July 22, 2024. Since then, Nvidia’s stock is up about 55 percent, compared to the S&P 500’s 32 percent. Micron, though, has soared about 937 percent since then.\nMicron makes memory and storage chips that help computers, phones, cars, data centers, and AI systems store and quickly access data.\nMake no mistake about it, memory stocks are starting to feel bubble-like. Micron has been one of the best-performing stocks in the S&P 500 this year, and after its latest earnings report, the stock jumped nearly 16 percent in a single day. Other memory chip companies, such as SK Hynix and Samsung, have also experienced massive runups. When an entire corner of the market starts moving like a meme stock, investors should slow down and ask whether the story has gotten ahead of the math. My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek , is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nI invested in the Invesco AI and Next Gen Software ETF (NOT an investment recommendation), which had 27 percent of its weighting in memory stocks. Each of the memory stocks in that ETF has way more than doubled. It has been a wild ride u…",
  "statement": "after its latest earnings report, the stock jumped nearly 16 percent in a single day"
 },
 {
  "id": "G254",
  "article_id": "gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32",
  "title": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewa",
  "passage": "…A trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about lockin…",
  "statement": "Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450"
 },
 {
  "id": "G481",
  "article_id": "gnews:bcd4e6c9e36d405b0efdd18e",
  "title": "Apple eyes Nvidia NVLink to power its new custom M8 Ultra AI servers — historically bitter rivals reportedly team up for 2029 data center push",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-17",
  "article_opening": "Apple eyes Nvidia NVLink to power its new custom M8 Ultra AI servers — historically bitter rivals reportedly team up for 2029 data center push\nBy Anton Shilov\nTime to bury the hatchet?\nApple is reportedly developing AI servers based on its own M-series processors and is evaluating NVLink Fusion technology for interconnects, according to The Information . The machines are expected to use M8 Ultra processors and arrive in 2029, the report claims.\nFor now, the usage of the NVLink Fusion platform is",
  "passage": "Apple eyes Nvidia NVLink to power its new custom M8 Ultra AI servers — historically bitter rivals reportedly team up for 2029 data center push\nBy Anton Shilov\nTime to bury the hatchet?\nApple is reportedly developing AI servers based on its own M-series processors and is evaluating NVLink Fusion technology for interconnects, according to The Information . The machines are expected to use M8 Ultra processors and arrive in 2029, the report claims.\nFor now, the usage of the NVLink Fusion platform is not formalized and has not been confirmed by either Apple or Nvidia — but an Apple decision to use it instead of competing solutions may have significantly broader market implications than just Apple using Nvidia hardware.\nApple is reportedly considering at least two server configurations: a smaller machine equipped with two M8 Ultra processors and a higher-end version featuring four M8 Ultra system-on-chips. Although Apple has its own UltraFusion technology for stitching two high-end SoCs together seamlessly, it looks like the company does not ha…",
  "statement": "Apple is reportedly developing AI servers based on its own M-series processors and is evaluating NVLink Fusion technology for interconnects, according to The Information"
 },
 {
  "id": "G225",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…Our second scenario analysis illustrates how the backstop math would work with a 6-year fixed price offtake. This scenario is admittedly an oxymoron – if you can get a 6-year fixed price offtake, then you of course do not need an Nvidia backstop and would thus would certainly not want to pay the revenue share out to Nvidia. Turning around and renting out compute on a 6-year contract would also be against the spirit of Nvidia’s backstop program as this deal would do nothing to further the objective of supplying compute for shorter tenors to a variety of different buyers, especially emerging workloads.\nIt is important to highlight that while Nvidia does have meaningful internal compute needs, neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop, and thus it is not surprising that the backstop levels would result in a typical Neocloud earning a zero or slightly negative project IRR.\nIn the table below, we illustrate the economics of renting to Nvidia under the backstop. This scenario would only materialize if the Neocloud is unable to find sufficient third-party customers at market-clearing rental and must activate the backstop.\nThat said, this scenario is exactly why the structure is financeable. Though the IRRs earned under this scenario will turn out to be near zero or even slightly negative, the Neocloud will at least still be able to cover debt payments, making lenders comfortable with this structure. Loan underwriting for clu…",
  "statement": "neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop"
 },
 {
  "id": "G392",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips used for model training, inference chips generally consume less power and are designed to meet the growing demand for AI computing.\nThe move marks a major strategic shift for DeepSeek, which has so far been known for focusing on AI model development rather than the semiconductor business.\nIf successful, the company would join a number of global AI developers that have begun designing their own chips to gain gr…",
  "statement": "DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei"
