 {
  "id": "G271",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "…eps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\nNVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18 and a high of $236.26. Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\nAbout 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not onl…",
  "statement": "Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%."
 },
 {
  "id": "G015",
  "article_id": "gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a",
  "title": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reach",
  "passage": "…e visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\nINTC Technical Setup — Trendline Defense at $140, RSI 55.21, Target $159.90\nOn the 2H chart, INTC is holding the ascending multi-touch black trendline after a series of rejections of the descending red trendlines from the $169 plus highs and…",
  "statement": "Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating"
 },
 {
  "id": "G372",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…system has been delayed,…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nA 78-layer PCB midplane delays NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 to 2028, dropping NVDA 1.4%, while AMD gains a clear competitive window with shares up 158% YTD.\nGoogle Cloud revenue surged 63% with backlog nearly doubling to $460 billion, strengthening its in-house TPU case as NVIDIA's Kyber slip extends to 2028.\nThe AI bottleneck has shifted from chip design to system assembly, yet NVIDIA still commands 81% market share and trades 45% below GuruFocus fair value.\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028. CNBC cited the SemiAnalysis post. NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) shares actually rose more than 1% on Monday — helped by the company’s denial and a Goldman Sachs note calling the valuation compelling — leaving its market cap around $4.7 trillion. Overseas, PCB suppliers to NVIDIA came under pressure, with reports of sharp intraday declines in names such as Japan’s Ibiden and Hong Kong’s Kingboard Laminates Holdings.\nKyber represents NVIDIA’s shift from selling chips to selling complete AI infrastructure. A single Kyber NVL144 cabinet houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs oper…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028"
 },
 {
  "id": "G217",
  "article_id": "gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "For a long time, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) was priced above $200 per share. Now, it has fallen below that after a few days of heavy selling pressure. The stock is down around 16% from its highs and is up a mere 6% for the year. That's a pretty disappointing result for most investors since Nvidia has been such a strong stock pick over the past few years.\nHowever, I still think the stock still has a bright future. In fact, I think it could easily rise over 50% to reach $300. But when might that occur",
  "passage": "…n in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\nThat sounds like further revenue growth ahead, and that's exactly what Wall Street analysts are projecting as well. For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%. Those are strong growth rates and will likely lead to major profits as well.\nFor fiscal 2028, ending January 2028, the average analyst projects $12.76 in earnings per share. I think that's a conservative estimate, because the analyst community has consistently underprojected Nvidia's growth.\nIf we use that figure and value the stock at a reasonable earnings multiple of 25, the projected share price at the end of fiscal 2028 is $319. As a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 per share sometime in late 2027.\nThat's 50% upside in about a year and a half, which is a great return in a short time frame . Furthermore, most of this growth is already known because management likely has good information on customer orders over the next year. So I think Nvidia is an excellent buy on this most recent round of sell-offs.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 6, 2026 at 8:53AM EST",
  "statement": "As a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 per share sometime in late 2027."
 },
 {
  "id": "G497",
  "article_id": "gnews:6a13316afb2f7768f3f2945c",
  "title": "OPEC Expected to Keep Oil Production Quotas Unchanged",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-30",
  "article_opening": "By Charles Kennedy - Sep 30, 2026, 8:30 AM CDT\nThe key OPEC+ producers are set to leave their current crude oil output quotas unchanged for November at a meeting this weekend, anonymous sources familiar with the plans told Reuters on Wednesday.\nThe Gulf producers in OPEC+ have been producing and exporting much lower volumes sin ce the Iran war crippled traffic through the Strait of Hormuz.\nIn the early September monthly meeting, Saudi Arabia, Russia, Iraq, Kuwait, Kazakhstan, Algeria, and Oman d",
  "passage": "…di Arabia and Russia, appears to have shifted its focus to debates and discussions about the 2027 quotas, which the alliance plans to review and set the baselines for any cuts going forward.\nIn recent weeks, OPEC’s oil production fell in August due to lower Saudi supply, but the output at the core OPEC+ producers increased.\nSaudi Arabia’s oil production fell in August amid Houthi threats to shipments in the Red Sea, dragging OPEC’s output down, a Bloomberg survey showed .\nBut OPEC’s data in its Monthly Oil Market Report (MOMR) for September showed that total crude oil production in the whole OPEC+ alliance averaged 38.05 million bpd in August 2026, up by 300,000 bpd higher compared to July.\nProduction at OPEC+ is about 5 million bpd lower compared to pre-war levels, as almost all OPEC Gulf members are trailing behind their quotas due to the ongoing shipping disruptions.\nBy Charles Kennedy for Oilprice.com\nForeign Investors Pull $3.2 Billion From Indian Markets as Oil Rally Returns\nAnalysts Cut China's Q4 Crude Import Forecasts by 400,000 Bpd",
  "statement": "Production at OPEC+ is about 5 million bpd lower compared to pre-war levels"
 },
 {
  "id": "G222",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…ay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\nIn 2025, we wrote extensively about how datacenter capacity was the bottleneck for AI compute growth. By early 2026, the datacenter supply situation improved considerably, but it became clear that chip production was now the limiting constraint. Now – mid-way through the year – it is clear that financing will now be one of the most significant obstacles to ramping large scale compute broadly available to everyone.\nThis is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\nThe Neocloud is of course free to rent to any other customer they would like for any term length they deem commercially reasonable and indeed the intent is for the Neocloud to never actually have to invoke the backstop.\nNvidia’s backstop program has a few key objectives:\nTo broaden compute availability: This objective has two dimensions, to open up the compute market well beyond just a few large hyperscalers and AI labs and to ensure rental contracts of varying contract terms (tenor) are available and not just 5y terms – particularly short terms of less than 1 year,\nSup…",
  "statement": "Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity"
 },
 {
  "id": "G391",
  "article_id": "gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a",
  "title": "DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above ",
  "passage": "…compute cost. It comes weeks after the company closed a record $7.5 billion funding round — its first outside capital — at a valuation exceeding $50 billion, with backing from Tencent, CATL, and founder Liang Wenfeng himself [4] .\nDeepSeek's chip targets the fastest-growing segment of AI computing. Inference workloads — running trained models for end users — now account for the majority of AI chip demand as companies deploy models at scale. Custom inference chips are typically cheaper and more power-efficient than the general-purpose GPUs sold by Nvidia, making them attractive for companies seeking to compress costs [2] .\nThe strategy aligns with DeepSeek's broader cost-efficiency playbook. In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] . Controlling its own silicon would give DeepSeek further leverage to undercut rivals on inference pricing.\nDesigning a competitive AI chip typically takes years and billions of dollars in investment. For a Chinese company, the challenge is compounded by U.S. export controls that bar access to the most advanced overseas foundries and restrict shipments of high-bandwidth memory — a component critical to inference chip performance [2] .\nAnalyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outs…",
  "statement": "In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%"
 },
 {
  "id": "G010",
  "article_id": "gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a",
  "title": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reach",
  "passage": "…urrently reading 55.21, positioning it as neutral-bullish, while green candles continue to soak up sell orders in close proximity to the trendline-resistance alignment. This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today.\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the rati…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel"
 },
 {
  "id": "G027",
  "article_id": "gnews:e26afcac61813d4e7af1b802",
  "title": "'Big Short' Michael Burry revealed fresh bets against Tesla, Nvidia, Caterpillar, and a chip index",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "By Theron Mohamed\nMichael Burry has placed fresh bets against Tesla, Caterpillar, Nvidia, Applied Materials, and an index of microchip stocks.\nThe investor of \"The Big Short\" fame, best known for predicting and profiting from the collapse of the mid-2000s housing bubble, revealed his latest shorts in a Substack post on Tuesday afternoon.\nBurry said he refreshed his wager against the iShares Semiconductor ETF (SOXX), purchasing bearish put options expiring in March 2027 instead of January 2027, w",
  "passage": "…as roughly quadrupled from its low last April, surging from around $160 to $640. It has doubled in value these past six months as investors have bet the AI boom will keep fueling insatiable demand for microchips .\nBurry published a chart showing the index that SOXX tracks, the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index, is the most extended it's been relative to its 200-day moving average since the dot-com bubble .\n\"The SOXX itself is a pure form of overvaluation in an index, a form that is rarely seen and never so easily recognized as such,\" he wrote.\nBurry said that he maintained his QQQ puts — wagers against the tech-heavy Nasdaq 100 — and shorted Tesla, Caterpillar, Nvidia, and Applied Materials.\nNone of the companies Burry said he is shorting immediately responded to requests for comment from Business Insider.\nTesla shares have rallied 22% from their April low to around $420. Burry, who's previously shorted Elon Musk's automaker, said he was \"happy it jumped back to this level.\"\nBurry said he's never shorted Caterpillar before, and owning shares of the maker of construction and mining equipment has \"always done great\" for him in the past.\n\"I am a bit shocked I am short CAT but this is just not anywhere near supported by the actual business,\" he wrote in a comment on his Substack.\nCaterpillar stock jumped by 86% in the first half of this year, and 167% over the past 12 months, partly because the company is seen as a major beneficiary of the AI infrastructure buildout .\nBurry poured cold water on T…",
  "statement": "None of the companies Burry said he is shorting immediately responded to requests for comment from Business Insider."
 },
 {
  "id": "G023",
  "article_id": "gnews:c3c81e4fb573a723e4081dd0",
  "title": "One of America's oldest manufacturers says AI is creating jobs — not replacing them",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "By Amanda Macias Fox News\nInside Corning's North Carolina factory, workers are producing the optical fiber that has become a critical piece of the infrastructure powering the AI boom. (Footage courtesy of Corning)\nBefore Henry Ford rolled out the Model T, before the Wright brothers took to the skies and before the Statue of Liberty welcomed millions to America's shores, Corning was already charting a course of innovation that continues today.\nBest known to many Americans for the glass products t",
  "passage": "…rth Carolina, facility told Fox News Digital.\nShe said employees are seeing firsthand how demand for AI platforms is driving the need for Corning's products.\n\"Right now, the world needs our glass optical fiber to support the AI demand we're seeing,\" Capek said. \"It's a great feeling on the factory floor.\"\nHer comments reflect a much broader trend. While much of the attention surrounding AI has focused on chipmakers and software developers, companies like Corning are supplying the glass technology that connects AI systems and supports the industry's rapid expansion. That investment is already translating into the opening of new U.S. factories and creation of American jobs.\nEarlier this year, NVIDIA partnered with Corning to build three advanced optical manufacturing facilities in North Carolina and Texas. It's expected to create more than 3,000 jobs across both states while expanding Corning's U.S. optical manufacturing capacity tenfold.\nHaving worked with innovators such as Apple co-founder Steve Jobs during his career, Weeks said he's proud to now partner with NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang as Corning helps build the infrastructure powering the AI revolution.\nWhile companies like NVIDIA have become synonymous with artificial intelligence, Weeks said many people overlook the role optical fiber plays in connecting AI systems.\n\"The common story is AI being powered by chips, but actually, those chips are connected by glass,\" Weeks said.\nThe AI manufacturing boom isn't just fueling growth at longtime…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA partnered with Corning to build three advanced optical manufacturing facilities in North Carolina and Texas"
 },
 {
  "id": "G032",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45",
  "title": "NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balanc",
  "passage": "…ion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.",
  "statement": "NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares"
 },
 {
  "id": "G326",
  "article_id": "gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated] | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\nBy Luke James\nLeaving Nvidia without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra past its current",
  "passage": "…edly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\nBy Luke James\nLeaving Nvidia without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra past its current Oberon rack.\nUpdate 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \"Our roadmap is intact.\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\nOriginal Story:\nNvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backplane. Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\nThe orthogonal backplane sits between Kyber's vertically mounted compute trays and the switch trays behind them, replacing the cable harnesses of earlier racks with a rigid board that carries the all-copper NVLink fabric. Kyber runs liquid cooling by default and stacks 144 Rubin Ultra packages, double the 72 packages in a …",
  "statement": "Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X."
 },
 {
  "id": "G105",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…t to coal under the banner of “energy security” – especially as surging demand from industries like electronics intensifies.\nNow, Nvidia and the global AI frenzy are locking Taiwan and South Korea deeper into fossil fuel dependency and chronic energy insecurity. The AI boom is fast becoming a carbon trap, and it is the people in East Asia who will be unable to escape worsening pollution and runaway carbon emissions.\nWorse still, while Nvidia quietly drives this environmental destruction in East Asia, it is actively trying to blindfold its own investors. Ahead of its June 24 annual meeting, shareholders proposed that Nvidia report on the lifecycle carbon emissions of its products after sale. The company recommended shareholders vote against the proposal, claiming it is “too prescriptive .”\nNvidia likes to brand itself as a climate champion, but its business model relies on a form of climate colonialism – outsourcing the dirtiest parts of its supply chain to East Asia while actively suppressing transparency.\nIf Jensen Huang truly wants to lead the future, Nvidia must come clean. It must disclose its full lifecycle emissions and legally commit, alongside its Asian manufacturing partners, to producing 100 percent carbon-free AI chips by 2030. Until then, its AI supremacy will remain a dirty empire built on East Asian coal and gas.\nGreenpeace East Asia is part of a global network of independent campaigning organizations dedicated to addressing…",
  "statement": "The company recommended shareholders vote against the proposal"
 },
 {
  "id": "G235",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…et is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\nAI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\nThe surge in borrowing requirements to date has driven by briskly growing demand from AI labs and hyperscalers, and the construction of AI clusters to service this demand was made financeable by long-term take-or-pay compute contracts backed by hyperscalers, with the most common offtake period being 5 years. Though this article will mainly focus on financing AI IT Capex - that is, GPUs and related capex like storage, networking and CPUs attached - there is much that also needs to be done to support the growth of datacenter capex financing. A much more detailed breakdown of both AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex can be found in our AI TCO Model and AI Datacenter Model respectively.\nExecuti…",
  "statement": "Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout."
 },
 {
  "id": "G273",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "…nium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parall…",
  "statement": "Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business"
 },
 {
  "id": "G457",
  "article_id": "gnews:07610e55de6cc86b2b508b1d",
  "title": "NVIDIA Nemotron Achieves Benchmark-Leading Performance With LangChain Deep Agents Harness",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "Tuning the LangChain Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra delivers leading performance and faster task completion on an open stack that enterprises can run, customize and control.\nNVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra is offering leading performance at lower cost than top closed models with the largest and most widely adopted AI agent orchestration platform.\nLangChain tuned its Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving the highest accuracy among open models, while completing more t",
  "passage": "Tuning the LangChain Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra delivers leading performance and faster task completion on an open stack that enterprises can run, customize and control.\nNVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra is offering leading performance at lower cost than top closed models with the largest and most widely adopted AI agent orchestration platform.\nLangChain tuned its Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving the highest accuracy among open models, while completing more tasks at higher throughput and running at 10x lower inference cost per run than leading closed models.\nMeasured against LangChain’s Deep Agents benchmark, Nemotron 3 Ultra also achieved business task parity with the highest-scoring closed models. No model retraining was required. Every gain came from engineering the environment around the model, not the model itself.\nAt a tenth of the cost, teams harnessing NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra can run evaluations continuously, experiment faster and build specialized agents across more of their business.\nLangChain’s agent engineering platform has more than 200 million monthly downloads. By tuning its Deep Agents harness specifically for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, it allows for high-performing agents that complete more tasks, run faster and give enterprises…",
  "statement": "running at 10x lower inference cost per run than leading closed models"
 },
 {
  "id": "G003",
  "article_id": "gnews:9a13ec8bfdc32e585c1f0604",
  "title": "Why NN (NNBR) Is Up 24.7% After Pre-Selling New NVIDIA Liquid Cooling Capacity Expansion",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "In late June 2026, NN, Inc. announced a major expansion of its liquid cooling production for NVIDIA AI data center racks, adding 47 new machine centers in its Wuxi, China plant with 100% of this new capacity already pre-sold and tied to multi-year, multi-product awards.\nThis move effectively deepens NN's role in NVIDIA's Asia supply chain while repurposing legacy automotive capacity and targeting US$80–US$90 million of accretive new business in 2026.\nWe'll now examine how NN's fully pre-sold exp",
  "passage": "…A-related liquid cooling capacity could reshape the company's existing investment narrative.\nWe've uncovered the 10 dividend fortresses yielding 5%+ that don't just survive market storms, but thrive in them.\nTo own NN, I think you have to believe its pivot from slower automotive and industrial markets toward higher value data center content can meaningfully change the story, even while the company is still unprofitable and carries meaningful debt. The fully pre sold NVIDIA liquid cooling expansion directly reinforces the near term growth catalyst of new program ramp ups, but it does not remove the execution and balance sheet risks that remain front and center.\nAmong the recent developments, NN's removal from the Russell Microcap Value Index in late June 2026 stands out alongside this NVIDIA news. Index removal can affect trading liquidity and share price volatility at the same time management is leaning into capital intensive growth tied to AI racks, which could sharpen the contrast between improving commercial momentum and ongoing funding, refinancing, and execution risks as the data center opportunity scales.\nRead the full narrative on NN (it's free!)\nNN's narrative projects $485.5 million revenue and $47.9 million earnings by 2028.\nUncover how NN's forecasts yield a $5.75 fair value , a 60% upside to its current price.\nYet while some analysts were already assuming NN could lift revenue to about US$489.5 million and earnings to roughly US$48.…",
  "statement": "NN's removal from the Russell Microcap Value Index in late June 2026"
 },
 {
  "id": "G444",
  "article_id": "gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c",
  "title": "TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24%\n$NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203\n52-Week High: $236\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nTD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed.\nThe call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua ",
  "passage": "…ross industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\nAll of that points to demand outpacing supply.\nNvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story.\nThe company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\nData center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\nNvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\nBeyond the CPU expansion, Nvidia has been striking new partnerships, including a deal with Valar Atomics to build a nuclear-powered data center in Utah aimed at reducing water use for AI computing.\nAI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\nSee analysts’ growth forecasts and price targets for Nvidia stock (It’s free) >>>\nWhat the Market Is Telling Us About Nvidia Stock\nTD Cowen’s bullish stance reflects growing confidence that AI spending is durable, not a temporary spike, and that it’s spreading across more customers and use cases than investors currently give Nvidia credit for.\nSome valuation analyses even suggest that Nvidia stock remains undervalued relative to its fair value.\nThat said, not everyone shares this optimism.\nInvestor Michael Burry has taken a short position against Nvidia stock, cit…",
  "statement": "a deal with Valar Atomics to build a nuclear-powered data center in Utah"
 },
 {
  "id": "G400",
  "article_id": "gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c",
  "title": "China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\nThe move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip in",
  "passage": "…y 8 , part of a broader chip rally. It marked the stock’s biggest one-day rise in more than a month.\nThough Nvidia is now up 9.4 per cent in 2026 , that pales in comparison with the gains of most major semiconductor peers.\nIn a statement, Chinese embassy spokesperson Liu Chang said that the country’s position on US chip exports has been consistent.\n“We advocate that China and the United States achieve mutual benefit and win-win outcomes through cooperation, and we oppose the politicisation, instrumentalisation, and weaponisation of technological and economic issues,” he said. “We stand ready to work with all parties to jointly safeguard the stability of global industrial and supply chains.”\nA representative for Santa Clara, California-based Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\nThe H200 import snags hinged in part on Chinese concerns that a flood of American-designed AI processors would hinder the government’s long-held goal of developing an indigenous chip industry. Beijing has also cited fears that allowing American-made chips into the local market could pose a cybersecurity risk.\nBut soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\nLabs in China have been struggling, like their US counterparts, to find enough computing power to meet their growing needs to train and run new models.\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper …",
  "statement": "A representative for Santa Clara, California-based Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment."
 },
 {
  "id": "G030",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45",
  "title": "NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balanc",
  "passage": "…press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\nThe promise of this new…",
  "statement": "Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G500",
  "article_id": "gnews:abe3ac14b6a21b7102c1ba59",
  "title": "Nvidia raises the Shield TV Pro price by $100—seven years after launch - How-To Geek",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-02",
  "article_opening": "By Jon Fingas\nNvidia might have released the Shield TV Pro in 2019, but even that device isn't immune to market pressure. The company has raised the price of the Shield TV Pro to $300, a $100 hike.\nThe company explains the move to 9to5 Google as the result of the technology cost crisis prompted by the AI data center rush. \"The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry,\" Nvidia says in a statement.\nNvidia has simultaneously discontinued the standard Shi",
  "passage": "… raised the price of the Shield TV Pro to $300, a $100 hike.\nThe company explains the move to 9to5 Google as the result of the technology cost crisis prompted by the AI data center rush. \"The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry,\" Nvidia says in a statement.\nNvidia has simultaneously discontinued the standard Shield TV. The Android TV hardware has been sold out for months, but it's now clear no new stock is coming. The firm hasn't said if there will be any future Shield products.\nNot every store is reflecting the Shield TV Pro price increase, so you'll want to buy quickly if you're interested.\nOther brands have already increased their prices. The Apple TV 4K jumped from $129 to $199, while the Google TV Streamer surged from $100 to $150. Even Roku's typically affordable streaming devices are more expensive, starting at $40 (previously $30) for the Streaming Stick .\nIs the Shield TV Pro still a good deal?\nThe Shield TV Pro is old, but its powerful-for-the-time Tegra X1+ chip has kept it relevant for years. It can play 4K HDR video, upscale lower-resolution content, and play games either from the Google Play Store or through the cloud using GeForce Now . It can double as a Plex media server .\nBy Tim Brookes\nThe higher price nonetheless hurts its value. You're now paying $300 for the media streamer when newer boxes cost much less, and might be better for gaming and other more demanding tasks. And while Nvidia is still updating…",
  "statement": "The Apple TV 4K jumped from $129 to $199, while the Google TV Streamer surged from $100 to $150."
 },
 {
  "id": "G097",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…mental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, which stated that energy is the foundational layer of AI development.\nThe activists urged the world’s biggest chip designer to fix the massive environmental damage it is causing in Taiwan, a critical manufacturing base for Nvidia that hosts more than 90 percent of global leading-edge chip manufacturing.\nRiding the wave of the global AI frenzy led by generative AI breakthroughs, Nvidia’s AI GPU microchips have made it the world’s most valuable company, and Huang one of the wealthiest people on the planet. For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated .\nIn the United States and other countries, anger is rising at the prodigious energy and water demands of gargantuan AI data centers. But there is a hidden cost: the toxic supply chain that manufactures advanced semiconductors that fill those data centers. Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled , and the trend is likely to continue as the company’s GPU shipments are forecast to grow at 26 percent per year.\nNvidia outsources most of its manufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GP…",
  "statement": "For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated"
 },
 {
  "id": "G215",
  "article_id": "gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "For a long time, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) was priced above $200 per share. Now, it has fallen below that after a few days of heavy selling pressure. The stock is down around 16% from its highs and is up a mere 6% for the year. That's a pretty disappointing result for most investors since Nvidia has been such a strong stock pick over the past few years.\nHowever, I still think the stock still has a bright future. In fact, I think it could easily rise over 50% to reach $300. But when might that occur",
  "passage": "… have told investors that the risk of underspending far outweighs the risk of overspending. The market may be growing frustrated by lofty capital expenditures on data centers, but the hyperscalers aren't planning on slowing down anytime soon.\nDuring its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\nThat sounds like further revenue growth ahead, and that's exactly what Wall Street analysts are projecting as well. For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%. Those are strong growth rates and will likely lead to major profits as well.\nFor fiscal 2028, ending January 2028, the average analyst projects $12.76 in earnings per share. I think that's a conservative estimate, because the analyst community has consistently underprojected Nvidia's growth.\nIf we use that figure and value the stock at a reasonable earnings multiple of 25, the projected share price at the end of fiscal 2028 is $319. As a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 per share sometime in late 2027.\nThat's 50% upside in about a year and a half, which is a great return in a short time frame . Furthermore, most of this growth is already known because management likely has good information on customer orders over the next year. So I think Nvidia is an excellent buy on this most recent round of sell-offs.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 6, 2026 at 8:53AM…",
  "statement": "For fiscal 2028, ending January 2028, the average analyst projects $12.76 in earnings per share."
 },
 {
  "id": "G389",
  "article_id": "gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a",
  "title": "DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above ",
